{
  "track": "es",
  "level": "A1",
  "unit": 13,
  "title": "Ordering food",
  "description": "Order a meal, and say what you want with it.",
  "explanation": {
    "summary": "Ordering is simple once you know two small words: con, with, and sin, without. Everything else is the vocabulary of a café, and the rule that an order takes un or una.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Con and sin",
        "body": "Con is with and sin is without. Both go straight after the thing they modify: un café con leche, un café sin azúcar. They are used for what comes with a dish or a drink, not for people: con mi hermana means with my sister.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "Un café con leche, por favor.",
            "en": "A coffee with milk, please."
          },
          {
            "es": "Un té sin azúcar.",
            "en": "A tea without sugar."
          },
          {
            "es": "Una ensalada con tomate.",
            "en": "A salad with tomato."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "What you order takes an article",
        "body": "In an order the noun takes un or una, never el: quiero un café, quiero una tortilla. The article says you mean one of them, which is exactly what an order is.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "Quiero una tortilla española.",
            "en": "I want a Spanish omelette."
          },
          {
            "es": "Para mí, una sopa de verduras.",
            "en": "For me, a vegetable soup."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Asking what there is",
        "body": "¿Qué hay? asks what there is. A waiter answers con una tortilla de patatas y una ensalada. The word for the bill, which you already know, is la cuenta.",
        "examples": [
          {
            "es": "¿Qué hay?",
            "en": "What is there?"
          },
          {
            "es": "Hay sopa de verduras y pescado.",
            "en": "There is vegetable soup and fish."
          },
          {
            "es": "La cuenta, por favor.",
            "en": "The bill, please."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
